  approaches → design → spec, and no code until you approve.

  What's here today: a static browser page ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m + [38;5;153mapp.js[39m) with a login form whose [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub, and a
  separate, unrelated Node script ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m) that prints a greeting. No build tooling, no tests, no
  framework, no backend.

  [1mThe fork:[0m where preferences live decides everything downstream — whether this needs a backend at all, what the
  read/write interface looks like, whether settings follow a person or a device, and whether any of it survives a
  cleared browser.

  - [1mBrowser [38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — zero new infrastructure, works with the static page as-is, synchronous API. Costs: per-device
    and per-browser, gone when site data is cleared, ~5MB cap, and nothing sensitive can go in it (readable by any
    script on the origin). Cheap to change later if the shape is kept behind a small module.
  - [1mServer-backed per account[0m — settings follow the user to any device and survive a cleared browser. Costs: requires a
    real backend and real auth, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is currently a stub returning [38;5;153m{success: true}[39m for any input — so this pulls
    a genuine auth story into scope. Expensive to add later only in that it needs the same backend either way; the
    client interface can stay identical.
  - [1mJSON file on disk[0m — fits the Node side ([38;5;153msrc/[39m), not the browser page. Only right if "sessions" means CLI runs, not
    page loads.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m behind a small preferences module[0m — one narrow interface ([38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m/[38;5;153mgetAll[39m/defaults)
  that the rest of the app talks to. It matches what the repo actually is right now, and because the storage backend
  sits behind that interface, swapping in a server later is a change to one file rather than to every call site. I'd
  explicitly rule out putting anything sensitive in it.
